● Here's how PdaNet+ works under the hood:

  Core mechanism: PdaNet runs an HTTP proxy server on the phone at 192.168.49.1:8000. Traffic from the client device routes through this proxy, which the phone then forwards as regular cellular data — masking tethering from the carrier.

  Connection modes:
  - USB — requires PdaNet desktop client on Windows/Mac
  - WiFi Direct — phone creates a hotspot, client sets proxy to 192.168.49.1:8000
  - Bluetooth — ~2 Mbps max

  Protocol details:
  - Standard HTTP proxy (port 8000)
  - Uses a PAC script: all traffic goes to 192.168.49.1:8000 except traffic to the gateway itself
  - Supports: HTTP, HTTPS, FTP, rsync
  - Doesn't support: ICMP/UDP (ping, traceroute, DNS over UDP)
  - Linux can use tun2socks to route all traffic through it system-wide

  Key limitation: DNS typically needs special handling since it's UDP.

  ---
  What kind of app are you building? A few possibilities:

  - An Android app that integrates with or extends PdaNet's functionality
  - A Linux/desktop client that connects through the PdaNet proxy automatically
  - An app that needs to detect and configure itself to work over a PdaNet connection
  - Something else entirely

  Knowing the platform and goal will shape the approach significantly.

