❯ on windows it sets windows up as a virtual LAN 

● Right — that's the key difference. On Windows, PdaNet's desktop client creates a virtual network adapter (virtual LAN), so all traffic is routed transparently at the network level — no proxy needed, all protocols work. It's essentially a VPN tunnel.

  On Android clients, PdaNet's companion app does the same thing using Android's VpnService API — creates a tun interface and routes everything through it.

  For your standalone APK, you have two paths:

  Option A — Use wss:// + OkHttp proxy
  - Configure OkHttp with the proxy at 192.168.49.1:8000
  - Use wss:// (not ws://) — OkHttp supports CONNECT tunneling for SSL WebSockets
  - Simple to implement, but requires your backend to support WSS
  - ws:// plain won't tunnel correctly through PdaNet's proxy

  Option B — Embed a VpnService (virtual LAN approach)
  - Your app creates its own VPN tunnel using Android VpnService
  - Uses something like tun2socks to route all traffic through the PdaNet proxy
  - All protocols work — mirrors what PdaNet's companion app and Windows client do
  - More complex, requires the BIND_VPN_SERVICE permission + user approval dialog

  Which fits your situation better — do you control the backend (can use WSS), or do you need all protocols to work regardless?

  Sources:
  - https://github.com/square/okhttp/issues/4390
  - https://ably.com/topic/websockets-android

